1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,730
Every time it rains in New Orleans,
the water has nowhere to go.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,330
So the city pumps it out, uphill,
over its walls and into a lake.

3
00:00:09,128 --> 00:00:11,898
And all that draining is a big
reason the city keeps sinking.

4
00:00:12,493 --> 00:00:16,803
A hundred and thirty years ago, almost
none of New Orleans was below sea level.

5
00:00:16,993 --> 00:00:18,803
Today, about half of it is.

6
00:00:19,033 --> 00:00:21,483
Some neighborhoods sit
twelve feet below the sea.

7
00:00:22,374 --> 00:00:24,804
So why doesn't New
Orleans just stop pumping?

8
00:00:25,054 --> 00:00:27,174
And why does it depend
on one set of floodgates,

9
00:00:27,214 --> 00:00:28,304
far up the Mississippi?

10
00:00:30,657 --> 00:00:33,127
The first city stood on
the highest ground around:

11
00:00:33,257 --> 00:00:36,147
a natural ridge of river
silt, today's French Quarter.

12
00:00:36,357 --> 00:00:37,607
Behind it lay swamp.

13
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:42,140
Around nineteen hundred, the city started
draining that swamp with powerful new

14
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:45,350
pumps, and built whole new
neighborhoods where the swamp had been.

15
00:00:46,161 --> 00:00:49,791
But the swamp soil was full of
peat, made of old roots and plants.

16
00:00:50,241 --> 00:00:52,391
Once the water was gone, air got in.

17
00:00:52,561 --> 00:00:55,631
The peat rotted, the soil packed
down, and the ground dropped.

18
00:00:56,537 --> 00:00:59,547
The first sign showed up on a
city map in eighteen ninety-five.

19
00:01:00,237 --> 00:01:02,547
Strangely, it put the old
swamps at twenty feet.

20
00:01:02,817 --> 00:01:04,907
Its heights were measured
from a mark in Cairo,

21
00:01:04,957 --> 00:01:06,467
Illinois, far up the river.

22
00:01:06,657 --> 00:01:10,427
Correct for that, and those swamps were
already about a foot below sea level.

23
00:01:11,294 --> 00:01:14,344
Those same spots are now
five to seven feet below.

24
00:01:14,814 --> 00:01:18,044
Parts of eastern New Orleans are
twelve to fourteen feet down.

25
00:01:18,741 --> 00:01:22,761
Measured from a NASA research plane, some
areas were sinking up to two inches a

26
00:01:22,801 --> 00:01:23,191
year.

27
00:01:23,726 --> 00:01:24,636
And that's the trap.

28
00:01:24,726 --> 00:01:27,176
The lower the city sinks,
the harder it has to pump.

29
00:01:27,326 --> 00:01:29,376
And the harder it drains,
the lower it sinks.

30
00:01:32,181 --> 00:01:35,211
The machine that made it
possible was invented right here.

31
00:01:35,581 --> 00:01:39,701
In nineteen fifteen, a young city engineer
named Albert Baldwin Wood put in a giant

32
00:01:39,741 --> 00:01:42,931
screw pump, the most advanced
drainage pump of its time.

33
00:01:43,141 --> 00:01:45,331
Even the Dutch sent engineers to study it.

34
00:01:45,601 --> 00:01:46,751
Here's how it works.

35
00:01:47,261 --> 00:01:50,471
Rain runs into some sixty-eight
thousand drains in the street,

36
00:01:50,521 --> 00:01:52,281
then through fifteen
hundred miles of pipes,

37
00:01:52,321 --> 00:01:54,651
into about two hundred miles of canals.

38
00:01:54,921 --> 00:01:59,091
Then the pumps lift it up, into big canals
that carry it out to Lake Pontchartrain.

39
00:01:59,984 --> 00:02:03,374
The whole system can keep up with
about half an inch of rain an hour.

40
00:02:03,564 --> 00:02:06,274
When rain falls much faster
than an inch an hour,

41
00:02:06,404 --> 00:02:07,214
the streets flood.

42
00:02:08,062 --> 00:02:11,492
Pumps of his design still work
today, some about a century old.

43
00:02:11,642 --> 00:02:13,512
The biggest are fourteen feet across.

44
00:02:14,264 --> 00:02:15,814
And here's the strange part.

45
00:02:16,004 --> 00:02:19,924
Fifty-one of the city's hundred and twenty
drainage pumps run on twenty-five hertz

46
00:02:19,964 --> 00:02:20,514
power.

47
00:02:21,326 --> 00:02:24,116
That's an old standard the
national grid left behind long ago.

48
00:02:24,186 --> 00:02:26,306
So for a century, the city
made this power itself,

49
00:02:26,346 --> 00:02:27,616
with its own old turbines.

50
00:02:28,026 --> 00:02:29,696
One of them was installed
in nineteen fifteen.

51
00:02:30,026 --> 00:02:31,846
It spent much of its
last decade under repair,

52
00:02:31,886 --> 00:02:34,116
and in twenty twenty-two
it stopped nineteen times.

53
00:02:34,306 --> 00:02:38,076
Only in December twenty twenty-five did
a new power plant let the city retire it.

54
00:02:38,211 --> 00:02:39,961
When the pumps fail, the city floods.

55
00:02:40,411 --> 00:02:44,261
In August twenty seventeen, eight to
nine inches of rain fell in a few hours.

56
00:02:44,491 --> 00:02:46,831
No drainage system here
could keep up with that.

57
00:02:46,871 --> 00:02:48,221
But some pumps were down.

58
00:02:48,411 --> 00:02:50,261
The water board first
said they all worked.

59
00:02:50,371 --> 00:02:52,501
It wasn't true, and its
director stepped down.

60
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:56,760
But the flood that nearly destroyed
New Orleans didn't come from rain,

61
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:57,910
or from the river.

62
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:00,550
It came through walls that
were built to stop it.

63
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:01,310
We'll get there.

64
00:03:04,075 --> 00:03:05,365
Pumps handle the rain.

65
00:03:05,495 --> 00:03:08,665
But the biggest threat to New Orleans
is the river it was built for.

66
00:03:09,224 --> 00:03:11,834
The Mississippi has
changed course many times.

67
00:03:11,924 --> 00:03:14,364
And for the last century, it
has been trying to do it again,

68
00:03:14,404 --> 00:03:17,614
into a shorter, steeper
path: the Atchafalaya River.

69
00:03:18,055 --> 00:03:22,245
In eighteen fifty, the Atchafalaya took
less than a tenth of the river's water.

70
00:03:22,495 --> 00:03:24,765
By nineteen fifty, it took thirty percent.

71
00:03:25,515 --> 00:03:27,905
Engineers warned the Mississippi
could switch within decades.

72
00:03:28,981 --> 00:03:32,231
If it did, the river past
New Orleans could turn salty.

73
00:03:32,561 --> 00:03:35,351
The city could lose its drinking
water, much of its port,

74
00:03:35,481 --> 00:03:38,151
and the fresh water its
chemical plants depend on.

75
00:03:38,935 --> 00:03:40,825
In twenty twenty-three, we got a preview.

76
00:03:41,315 --> 00:03:44,995
Salt water crept up the river, and the
Army Corps built an underwater wall to hold

77
00:03:45,035 --> 00:03:45,585
it back.

78
00:03:45,855 --> 00:03:49,105
In September, the salt went over
it, and the wall had to be raised.

79
00:03:49,375 --> 00:03:52,955
Downriver, in Plaquemines Parish, the
drinking water was affected for more than two

80
00:03:52,995 --> 00:03:53,725
hundred days.

81
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:57,920
So in nineteen sixty-three, the Army
Corps of Engineers finished the Old River

82
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:02,590
Control Structure, far upriver: a
set of giant floodgates with one job.

83
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:04,960
Keep seventy percent of the
water in the Mississippi,

84
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,970
and let only thirty percent go.

85
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:11,120
In nineteen seventy-three, a
huge flood almost broke it.

86
00:04:11,230 --> 00:04:14,750
Part of a wall collapsed, and the water
dug deep holes on both sides of its

87
00:04:14,790 --> 00:04:15,360
foundation.

88
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:17,100
It held, but only just.

89
00:04:17,470 --> 00:04:20,070
Since then, it has never been
allowed to work as hard again,

90
00:04:20,110 --> 00:04:23,340
and in nineteen eighty-six a second
structure was added beside it.

91
00:04:25,925 --> 00:04:28,975
For the river's floods, the
city once had a harsher option.

92
00:04:29,165 --> 00:04:31,455
It protected itself by
flooding someone else.

93
00:04:32,063 --> 00:04:36,073
In nineteen twenty-seven, during one of
the worst floods in American history,

94
00:04:36,683 --> 00:04:38,703
engineers blew up the levee at Caernarvon,

95
00:04:38,743 --> 00:04:41,693
south of the city, with
thirty-nine tons of dynamite.

96
00:04:41,963 --> 00:04:44,433
About ten thousand people
had to leave their homes.

97
00:04:45,369 --> 00:04:47,099
They were promised full compensation.

98
00:04:47,529 --> 00:04:48,699
Thousands got nothing.

99
00:04:48,889 --> 00:04:51,319
And the blast wasn't
even needed: within days,

100
00:04:51,409 --> 00:04:53,279
levees broke upriver on their own.

101
00:04:53,672 --> 00:04:55,702
Today, a spillway does that job.

102
00:04:55,972 --> 00:04:59,632
The Bonnet Carré Spillway sends flood
water into Lake Pontchartrain before it

103
00:04:59,672 --> 00:05:00,562
reaches the city.

104
00:05:00,852 --> 00:05:02,682
It used to open once every few years.

105
00:05:02,792 --> 00:05:06,002
Then it opened three years in a row,
from twenty eighteen to twenty twenty.

106
00:05:06,352 --> 00:05:08,402
And in twenty nineteen,
for the first time ever,

107
00:05:08,452 --> 00:05:10,142
it had to open twice in one year.

108
00:05:12,412 --> 00:05:14,892
Everything so far was
about keeping water out.

109
00:05:14,932 --> 00:05:16,872
In two thousand five, it got in.

110
00:05:16,912 --> 00:05:19,602
Not just over the walls,
but straight through them.

111
00:05:20,314 --> 00:05:24,304
When Hurricane Katrina pushed its
surge into the city's drainage canals,

112
00:05:24,414 --> 00:05:27,364
the floodwall on the 17th
Street Canal gave way.

113
00:05:27,614 --> 00:05:29,484
The water hadn't even reached its top.

114
00:05:30,369 --> 00:05:32,499
In all, the system had
fifty major breaches.

115
00:05:32,829 --> 00:05:34,619
Most came from water pouring over the top.

116
00:05:34,789 --> 00:05:37,739
But four walls failed because
the ground beneath them gave way.

117
00:05:38,069 --> 00:05:40,209
On the 17th Street and
London Avenue canals,

118
00:05:40,249 --> 00:05:42,379
they broke before the
water ever reached the top.

119
00:05:42,629 --> 00:05:44,569
The water pushed the walls
away from their levees,

120
00:05:44,609 --> 00:05:46,959
a gap opened, and the
ground underneath gave out.

121
00:05:47,187 --> 00:05:49,177
About eighty percent of the city flooded,

122
00:05:49,407 --> 00:05:51,737
in many places more
than fifteen feet deep.

123
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:53,970
Most pump stations stopped.

124
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:56,990
Power was lost, many
operators had been evacuated,

125
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:59,930
and thirty-four of seventy-one
stations were damaged.

126
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:02,650
It took about six weeks
to pump the city dry.

127
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:05,690
In Louisiana, more than
a thousand people died.

128
00:06:06,608 --> 00:06:08,338
Many who left never came back.

129
00:06:08,428 --> 00:06:11,798
Today, the city has about a quarter
fewer people than in two thousand.

130
00:06:12,617 --> 00:06:16,457
In twenty-oh-six, the head of the Army
Corps of Engineers said the Corps took

131
00:06:16,497 --> 00:06:17,347
responsibility.

132
00:06:17,817 --> 00:06:21,717
He called it the first time the Corps had
to take responsibility for a catastrophic

133
00:06:21,757 --> 00:06:22,527
failure.

134
00:06:23,417 --> 00:06:25,167
Then it built a new wall around the bowl:

135
00:06:25,417 --> 00:06:28,417
levees, floodwalls, a surge
barrier almost two miles long,

136
00:06:28,457 --> 00:06:30,437
and giant gates where
those canals meet the lake,

137
00:06:30,477 --> 00:06:32,787
with pump stations that
push the rain over them.

138
00:06:33,077 --> 00:06:36,697
On the West Bank stands what the Corps
calls the largest drainage pump station in

139
00:06:36,737 --> 00:06:37,247
the world.

140
00:06:37,517 --> 00:06:39,567
Total cost: about fourteen
and a half billion dollars.

141
00:06:40,447 --> 00:06:43,337
But the new walls are sinking
too, and the sea keeps rising.

142
00:06:43,667 --> 00:06:46,777
The original design allowed
for one foot of sea-level rise.

143
00:06:47,067 --> 00:06:48,877
A newer study uses almost two.

144
00:06:49,147 --> 00:06:52,827
In twenty twenty-one, the Corps proposed
a three-point-two-billion-dollar plan to

145
00:06:52,867 --> 00:06:55,127
raise about a hundred and
fifteen miles of levees,

146
00:06:55,167 --> 00:06:56,657
just to keep the same protection.

147
00:06:59,095 --> 00:07:01,145
So why doesn't New
Orleans just stop pumping?

148
00:07:01,755 --> 00:07:03,485
There are really two kinds of pumping.

149
00:07:03,875 --> 00:07:05,805
One pulls groundwater out of wells.

150
00:07:05,895 --> 00:07:07,575
Where the city stopped
that, in the northeast,

151
00:07:07,615 --> 00:07:09,445
the ground has even risen a little.

152
00:07:09,575 --> 00:07:11,225
The other kind pumps out the rain.

153
00:07:11,335 --> 00:07:12,665
And that one can never stop.

154
00:07:13,646 --> 00:07:17,366
And even where the wells are gone, leaking
water pipes under the streets now act

155
00:07:17,406 --> 00:07:20,616
like hidden drains, pulling the
groundwater down on their own.

156
00:07:21,422 --> 00:07:23,392
But the rain still has to go somewhere.

157
00:07:23,642 --> 00:07:26,152
About half the city is
already below sea level.

158
00:07:26,302 --> 00:07:28,892
Switch off the drainage
pumps, and the bowl fills up.

159
00:07:29,661 --> 00:07:31,211
We know what that looks like.

160
00:07:31,541 --> 00:07:36,061
Southwest of the city, a drained farm called
Delta Farms lost its levee in nineteen

161
00:07:36,101 --> 00:07:38,471
sixty-one, and again in
nineteen seventy-one.

162
00:07:38,921 --> 00:07:40,411
Today, it's open water.

163
00:07:43,298 --> 00:07:46,098
So New Orleans pumps
every drop of rain uphill,

164
00:07:46,138 --> 00:07:48,228
on pumps built a century ago.

165
00:07:48,478 --> 00:07:51,038
It holds a river in place
with one set of floodgates,

166
00:07:51,078 --> 00:07:53,928
and lives inside a wall
that sinks along with it.

167
00:07:54,761 --> 00:07:56,391
Which city should we explain next?

168
00:07:56,501 --> 00:07:57,291
Tell me in the comments.

169
00:07:57,905 --> 00:08:01,785
Three hundred years ago, the first settlers
built on the only high ground they could

170
00:08:01,825 --> 00:08:03,135
find: the riverbank.

171
00:08:03,285 --> 00:08:04,435
It's still above the sea.

172
00:08:04,685 --> 00:08:08,155
Everything behind it stays dry
the same way it did a century ago.

173
00:08:08,365 --> 00:08:10,895
Every time it rains, someone
switches on the pumps.

174
00:08:11,305 --> 00:08:12,355
This was How It's Run.
